  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nAn area of precipitation is in western IL so far this morning and is \ndecaying as it is moving east. There will be multiple rounds of\nshowers and thunderstorms today as a cold front slowly pushes \ninto the state then stalls out later today. There is a marginal \nrisk for severe weather this afternoon, primarily for damaging \nwinds, but there is a 2% tornado risk west of I-55. \n\nIt looks like the prime time for severe weather will be this \nafternoon from 19z to 03z (2pm to 10pm). The environment is decently \nsupportive of severe. MUCAPE values (and similarly SBCAPE values \nwith a small cap) of 1500-2500 J/kg and bulk shear values of 30-40 \nknots. SRH values look to be around 200-300 mostly west of I-55 \nwhere the 2% tornado risk exists. Overnight, the severe threat \nshould wane without daytime heating. CAMs are depicting everything \ndissipating in eastern IL around 03-04z providing widespread dry \nconditions for a few hours tonight. The most extreme version of \nwidespread drying conditions in the CAMs is the RRFS. RRFS is \nshowing everything drying out by 00z and staying widely dry through \nabout 09-10z tonight. The next round of showers and thunderstorms \nmoves into southwest CWA looks to enter by 08-10z tonight. \n\nWPC has a slight risk ERO for today. The CAMs are showing some \ntraining today focused near/along the MO/IA border and extending \neast into the central part of IL. LPMM is showing pockets of 3-5 \ninches across central IL. We will need to monitor for a flash \nflooding threat.\n\nThe front is going to linger over the area into the weekend, keeping \nus in an active pattern. Each day, from Thursday to Monday, could \nsee some non-zero risks for severe weather (especially north of\nI-70), with the machine learning models pointing that way as \nwell. Saturday looks to be the driest day in the forecast. Check\nback with upcoming forecasts to see if any severe threats have \nincreased for the end of the week.\n\nHighs in the 80s and low in the 60s continue. Temperatures look to \nnear or rise above 90 degrees for highs by Sunday into Tuesday, with \nincreasing humidity. The LREF shows a 50-80% chance of apparent \ntemperatures being greater than 95 degrees on Monday afternoon and \n40-60% chance on Tuesday afternoon across the entire forecast area. \nThe CPC has both the 6-10 and 8-14 day outlooks with above normal \ntemperatures. This is said with the caveat: if we get widespread \nthunderstorm activity in the beginning part of the week, the heat \nindices may not be able to achieve 95-100+ degrees.",
